<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Entitlements for a LOCAL, UNSIGNED developer build only. Never ship this.

  The shipped DebugProfile/Release entitlements turn the App Sandbox on and bind the app to a
  team through `com.apple.application-identifier`. Both are right for a released build and both
  are unusable on a machine that is not in that team: without a provisioning profile for
  com.keplr.vizor the build is refused outright, and if the entitlements are simply dropped to
  get past that, the app launches sandboxed-without-an-identity and `flutter_secure_storage`
  cannot reach the Keychain at all — which surfaces as "Secure storage is locked" with a Retry
  button that can never succeed, because nothing about it is transient.

  So this file keeps the sandbox off. A non-sandboxed, ad-hoc-signed app can use the Keychain
  normally, which is all a devnet PoC needs. The cost: an ad-hoc signature is derived from the
  binary, so every rebuild produces a new identity and the previous build's Keychain items are no
  longer readable — a rebuilt app comes up as a fresh wallet. That is fine for regtest and would
  not be fine for anything else.
-->
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
